Lab Material
Lab 8: Managing Horizon (Dashboard)
Objectives
Configure and manage the Horizon web interface for OpenStack users and administrators
Implement secure access to OpenStack services through Horizon
Manage user permissions and roles within Horizon
Launch and manage instances and volumes using Horizon dashboard
Prerequisites
OpenStack environment with Horizon dashboard installed
Admin credentials for OpenStack
Basic understanding of OpenStack services
Web browser with JavaScript enabled
Terminal access to OpenStack controller node (for configuration tasks)
Lab Tasks
Task 1: Configure Horizon for Secure Access
Subtask 1.1: Enable HTTPS for Horizon
Connect to your OpenStack controller node via SSH:

ssh username@controller-node
Install the required packages:

sudo apt-get install apache2 libapache2-mod-wsgi-py3
Generate SSL certificates (or use existing ones):

sudo openssl req -x509 -nodes -days 365 -newkey rsa:2048 \
-keyout /etc/ssl/private/horizon.key \
-out /etc/ssl/certs/horizon.crt
Configure Apache for HTTPS: Edit /etc/apache2/sites-available/horizon.conf:

<VirtualHost *:443>
    SSLEngine on
    SSLCertificateFile /etc/ssl/certs/horizon.crt
    SSLCertificateKeyFile /etc/ssl/private/horizon.key
    WSGIScriptAlias / /usr/share/openstack-dashboard/openstack_dashboard/wsgi.py
    <Directory /usr/share/openstack-dashboard/openstack_dashboard>
        Require all granted
    </Directory>
</VirtualHost>
Enable the configuration and restart Apache:

sudo a2ensite horizon.conf
sudo systemctl restart apache2
Expected Outcome: Horizon dashboard should now be accessible via HTTPS at https://<controller-ip>

Troubleshooting Tip: If you get SSL errors, try clearing your browser cache or adding a security exception.

Task 2: Manage User Permissions and Roles
Subtask 2.1: Create a New User
Log in to Horizon as admin
Navigate to Identity > Users
Click Create User
Fill in details:
User Name: labuser
Email: labuser@example.com
Password: [set secure password]
Primary Project: demo
Role: _member_
Subtask 2.2: Assign Project Roles
Navigate to Identity > Projects
Select the demo project
Go to Project > Manage Members
Add labuser with role member
For admin privileges, add admin role
Expected Outcome: New user can log in with assigned permissions

Key Concept: OpenStack uses role-based access control (RBAC) to manage permissions

Task 3: Instance and Volume Management
Subtask 3.1: Launch an Instance
Log in as labuser
Navigate to Compute > Instances
Click Launch Instance
Configure:
Instance Name: test-vm
Source: Select an image (e.g., CirrOS)
Flavor: m1.tiny
Networks: Select available network
Click Launch Instance
Subtask 3.2: Create and Attach Volume
Navigate to Volumes > Volumes
Click Create Volume
Name: test-vol
Size: 1 GB
Once created, select Manage Attachments
Attach to test-vm
Expected Outcome: Instance runs with attached storage volume

Troubleshooting Tip: If instance fails to launch, check quota limits in Admin > Compute > Overview

Conclusion
In this lab, you have:

Configured Horizon dashboard for secure HTTPS access
Managed user permissions and role assignments
Successfully launched an instance and attached storage volumes
These skills are essential for OpenStack environments, particularly when preparing for Red Hat OpenShift certifications that build upon OpenStack knowledge.

